The Mercedes-Benz A220 CDI was part of the W176 generation, produced from 2012 to 2018. It was powered by a 2.1-litre diesel engine producing 170 hp and 350 Nm (258 lb-ft) of torque. It was primarily available with a 7-speed DCT automatic transmission, driving the front wheels, although a 4MATIC AWD version was also available.

Performance figures include a 0-60 mph acceleration time of around 7.5 to 8.2 seconds and a top speed of 135-137 mph. Fuel economy is a strong point for this diesel, with reported real-world averages around 33-53 mpg US (42.4 mpg average from user data) and official figures higher. CO2 emissions are relatively low, around 107-115 g/km. As a diesel, it has no electrification features.

Dimensions for the W176 A-Class are around 4292-4299 mm length, 1780 mm width (2022 mm including mirrors), and 1433-1434 mm height, with a wheelbase of 2699 mm. Cargo space is 341 liters with seats up, expanding to 1157 liters with seats folded. It has 5 seats. Ground clearance is around 93 mm (3.66 inches) or 113mm depending on measurement point and suspension. The turning circle is around 11 meters (36.09 ft) or 5.5 meters turning radius.

Reliability of the W176 generation can be mixed, with potential issues reported for the engine (chain tensioner, fuel delivery, turbocharger, DPF), DCT transmission, suspension components, braking system, and electrical issues like battery drain. Maintenance costs are typical for a Mercedes. Used prices in the UK for 2014-2015 models are typically in the £8,000 - £14,000 range. Insurance groups are around 25E.

The W176 A-Class received a 5-star Euro NCAP safety rating, with good scores for adult and child occupant protection. ADAS features were limited compared to modern cars, though some were available. Visibility can be average. Headlights were typically halogen or optional Bi-xenon/LED. Security features included alarm and immobiliser.

Infotainment systems in the W176 were less advanced than current models, typically featuring smaller screens (5.8-inch or 7-inch standard, up to 8-inch optional) and less intuitive interfaces compared to the latest MBUX system. Smartphone integration (Apple CarPlay/Android Auto) was introduced later in the W176's life or available as an upgrade. Audio systems were standard with options available. USB ports were present, but likely older types.

Comfort and convenience features were available, including optional heated seats, dual-zone climate control, parking assist, and cruise control.

Brand prestige is high (Mercedes-Benz). The A-Class is the entry-level model (high model prestige score). The A220 CDI was a mid-range diesel trim (average trim prestige score). Interior luxury was considered good for its time but is not up to current standards. Interior tech focus was lower than modern cars, though the facelift improved this.

The style was a significant departure from previous generations, being lower and sleeker, with a moderately aggressive front end, though now dated compared to the latest designs. Handling was generally praised, though the ride could be firm, especially with sport suspension. Cabin noise, particularly from the diesel engine, could be noticeable. Shift smoothness with the DCT was generally good. Steering feel was considered decent.

